<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Provence</h1><p class="st-meta">947 CE – 1481 CE (approx.) · capital Aix-en-Provence</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Mediterranean county between the Rhône and the Alps that passed from Catalan to Angevin counts and was bequeathed to France in 1481.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, Boso of Arles held comital power in Provence around the mid-10th century. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-provence">William the Liberator</a> drove the Saracens from their base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a> in 973 and became effective ruler of the region, founding the first comital dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1112 the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douce-i-countess-of-provence">Douce</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iii-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</a>, bringing Catalan counts who fostered troubadour culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-provence">Raymond Berengar IV</a>'s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-of-provence">Beatrice</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> in 1246, and the Angevin counts, also kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix</a> and Naples. The popes settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, on the county's edge, in the 14th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-i-of-naples">Queen Joanna I</a> adopted Louis I of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-anjou">Anjou</a>, whose line held Provence until 1481. That year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv-duke-of-anjou">Charles of Maine</a>, heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, bequeathed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>, and Provence was united with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>, while keeping its own estates and parliament at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-provence.jpg" alt="County of Provence" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provence_in_France_(1789).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>947 CE – 1481 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aix-en-Provence</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William the Liberator, Raymond Berengar IV of Provence, René of Anjou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Provence</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1214&amp;focus=county-of-provence" aria-label="County of Provence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 634 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Provence lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5131 890L5111 886M5131 890L5229 694M5131 890L4984 945M5131 890L5349 976M5131 890L5054 728M5131 890L5139 877M5131 890L5130 898"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5111 886h0M5229 694h0M4984 945h0M5349 976h0M5054 728h0M5139 877h0M5130 898h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5131 890h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5131 890h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aix-en-Provence. <a href="/atlas?year=1214&amp;focus=county-of-provence">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
